<start_of_turn>model
","The maximum volume of oxygen that can be taken from the air and supplied to the body's cells per minute is known as whole body oxygen delivery. Oxygen delivery is a critical parameter in assessing the efficiency of oxygen transport in the body.  

The maximum oxygen delivery is calculated using the following formula:  
\[
\text{Oxygen Delivery} = \text{Oxygen Uptake} = \text{VCO}_2 \times \text{CO}_2 \text{ to O}_2 ratio
\]  
where:  
- Oxygen uptake is the maximum rate at which oxygen can be taken from the air and supplied to the body's cells.  
- VCO2 is the volume of carbon dioxide produced per minute.  
- CO_2 to O_2 ratio is the ratio of carbon dioxide to oxygen, which is approximately 2.2.  

The maximum oxygen delivery is typically measured in the context of an pulmonary-physiological test, such as an electrocardiogram (ECGC) or a pulse-on-oxygen analyzer.  

