<USER_REQUEST>
Calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction in which 3 moles of carbon monoxide (CO) gas is burned with excess oxygen gas to form 3 moles of carbon dioxide (CO2) gas. The reaction is carried out at constant pressure and a temperature of 298 K.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΔS°(products) - ΔS°(reactants)

where ΔS° is the standard entropy change for each substance.

The reaction is:

CO(g) + 1/2 O2(g) → CO2(g)

Since we have 3 moles of CO burning, the reaction becomes:

3CO(g) + 3/2 O2(g) → 3CO2(g)

From standard thermodynamic tables, we can look up the standard entropies (S°) at 298 K for each compound:

S°(CO) = 201.6 J/(mol·K)
S°(O2) = 205.7 J/(mol·K)
S°(CO2) = 237.5 J/(mol·K)

Now, we can calculate the change in entropy for the reaction:

ΔS° = 3 * S°(CO2) - (3 * S°(CO) + 3/2 * S°(O2))

ΔS° = 3 * 237.5 J/(mol·K) - (3 * 201.6 J/(mol·K) + 3/2 * 205.7 J/(mol·K))

ΔS° = 712.5 J/(mol·K) - (584.8 J/(mol·K) + 308.5 J/(mol·K))

ΔS° = 712.5 J/(mol·K) - 893.3 J/(mol·K)

ΔS° = -180.8 J/(mol·K)

The change in entropy for the reaction is -180.8 J/(mol·K).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

