What is the relationship used to estimate the flammability limits of mixtures from the data for individual fuels?,"<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the relationship used to estimate the flammability limits of mixtures from the data for individual fuels?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The relationship used to estimate the flalling limits of mixtures from the data for individual fuels is the **Cokkovill-Harkov-Hansen (H.H.C.) rule**, also known as the **H.H.C. rule**.  

The H.H.C. rule is a empirical model that estimates the lower and upper flammance limits (LFL and UFL) of a mixture of gases by weighting the LFLs and UFLs of the individual components by their mole fractions.  

The equations are:  
\[
\text{Lower LFL} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( \frac{y_i}{1 - \frac{y_i}{LFL_i}} \right)
\]  
\[
\text{Upper UFL} = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \left( \frac{y_i}{1 - \frac{y_i}{UFL_i}} \right)
\]  
where:  
- \( n \) is the number of components in the mixture.  
- \( y_i \) is the mole fraction of component \( i \).  
- \( LFL_i \) and \( UFL_i \) are the lower and upper flammability limits of component \( i \), respectively.  

The H.H.C. rule is widely used in the chemical and chemical engineering industries for estimating the flammability limits of mixtures.  

\[
\boxed{\text{H.H.C. rule}}
\]",fluency,"# Task Description
