","To calculate the standard enthalpy change (ΔH°) for the reaction, we can use the formula:

ΔH° = Σ ΔH°(products) - Σ ΔH°(reactants)

In this case, the products are Ca(OH)2 (calcium hydroxide) and the reactants are CaO (calcium oxide) and H2O (water).

The standard enthalpies of formation (ΔH°) are given as:

ΔH°(CaO(s)) = -635.1 kJ/mol
ΔH°(H2O(l)) = -285.8 kJ/mol
ΔH°(Ca(OH)2(s)) = -986.2 kJ/mol

Now, we can plug these values into the formula:

ΔH° = [1 × ΔH°(Ca(OH)2(s))] - [1 × ΔH°(CaO(s)) + 1 × ΔH°(H2O(l))]

ΔH° = [1 × (-986.2 kJ/mol)] - [1 × (-635.1 kJ/mol) + 1 × (-285.8 kJ/mol)]

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol - (-635.1 kJ/mol - 285.8 kJ/mol)

ΔH° = -986.2 kJ/mol + 635.1 kJ/mol + 285.8 kJ/mol

ΔH° = -986.2 + 635.1 + 285.8 kJ/mol

ΔH° = -100.3 kJ/mol

