<start_of_turn>model
","a) **Series System**:
In a series system, the system fails if and only if one of its components fails. The reliability of the system is the product of the reliancies of all components.  
- Let \( R_i(t) \) be the reliability of the \( i \)-th component, where \( i = 1, \ldots, n \).  
- The system reliability \( R_S(t) \) is given by:
  \[
  R_S(t) = \prod_{i=1}^n R_i(t) = R_1(t) \cdot R_2(t) \cdot R_3(t) \cdot \cdots \cdot R_n(t)
  \]
  This means the system is reliable at time \( t \) if and only if all components are still working at time \( t \).  

b) **Parallel System**:
In a parallel system, the system is safe if only only if all components are working. The system fails if and only if all components fail.  
