</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To find the new repayment period, we use the formula for simple interest:  
\[
\text{Annual cost} = \frac{\text{Cost of car}}{\text{Number of years}}
\]  
Given the cost of the car is $6,000 and the original repayment period is 5 years, the annual cost is:  
\[
\text{Annual cost} = \frac{6000}{5} = 1200 \text{ dollars per year}
\]  
Since the cost is per year, the cost per month is:  
\[
\text{Monthly cost} = \frac{1200}{12} = 100 \text{ dollars per month}
\]  
Now, the new monthly cost is $150 more, so the new monthly cost is:  
\[
\text{New monthly cost} = 100 + 150 = 250 \text{ dollars per month}
\]  
The total cost of the car is $6,000, so the new number of years is:  
\[
\text{New number of years} = \frac{6000}{250 \times 12} = \frac{6000}{3000} = 2 \text{ years}
\]  
Thus, the new repayment period is $\boxed{2}$ years.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

