","The equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by the point-point form of the equation of a line:  
\[ y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a). \]  
This equation is derived from the definition of the derivative as the slope of the tangent line at a point. The derivative \( f'(a) \) is the slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \), and the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is on the line and on the graph of \( f \).  

Thus, the slope of the tangent line is \( f'(a) \), and the point on the line is \( (a, f(a)) \). Substituting these into the point-point form of the line equation gives the desired equation:  
\[ y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a). \]  

This equation represents the line tangent to the graph of \( f \) at \( (a, f(a)) \).  

Final answer:  
\[ \boxed{y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)} \]",fluency,"# Task Description
