- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength,
- \(n_1\) is the refractive index of the material where the evanescent wave is propagating,
- \(n_2\) is the refractive index of the material outside the core.

In this case, the evanescent wave is generated at the interface between the GaAs core and the Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)S cladding. The difference in refractive indices is given as \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index of the cladding and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the core.

Substituting the given values:
\[
\lambda = 1 \mu m = 1 \times 10^{-6} \text{ m},
\]
\[
n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3
\]

