<USER_REQUEST>
A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The force on the proton is given by:  
\[ F = qE = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N} \]  
The acceleration of the proton is then:  
\[ a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2 \]  
Rounding to two significant figures, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.8 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial speed is \(v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s}\). Using the kinematic equation \(v = v_0 + at\), we solve for \(t\):  
\[ t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0}{4.81 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.28 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s} \]  
Rounding to two significant figures, the time is \(\boxed{7.3 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

