","To calculate the recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the principle of momentum conservation. The total momentum before the cannonball is fired is zero (since the gun and Earth are at rest). After firing, the momentum of the cannonball must be equal in magnitude and opposite in direction to the momentum of the Earth.

1. **Momentum Conservation Equation:**
   \[
   m_{\text{cannonball}} v_{\text{cannonball}} = m_{\text{Earth}} v_{\text{Earth}}
   \]
   where:
   - \(m_{\text{cannonball}} = 1 \text{ kg}\) (mass of the cannonball)
   - \(v_{\text{cannonball}} = 90.0 \text{ m/s}\) (velocity of the cannonball)
   - \(m_{\text{Earth}} = 5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}\) (mass of the Earth)
   - \(v_{\text{Earth}} = 9.0 \times 10^{-11} \text{ m/s}\) (velocity of the Earth)

2. **Solve for \(v_{\text{Earth}}\):**
   \[
   v_{\text{Earth}} = \frac{m_{\text{cannonball}} v_{\text{cannonball}}}{m_{\text{Earth}}} = \frac{(1 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s})}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}} = 1.5 \times 10^{-22} \text{ m/s}
   \]

