The intensity of thermal radiation of a black body in terms of temperature \( T \) and wavelength \( \lambda \) is given by the **black body radiation law**, also known as the **Planck's law**:

\[
I(\lambda, T) = \frac{8 \pi h^2 c^2}{\lambda^4} \frac{1}{e^{hc/\lambda kT} - 1}
\]

where:
- \( I(\lambda, T) \) is the intensity of the radiation at wavelength \( \lambda \) and temperature \( T \).
- \( h \) is the Planck's constant, approximately \( 6.626 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{s} \).
- \( c \) is the speed of light, approximately \( 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \).
- \( \lambda \) is the wavelength of the radiation.
- \( T \) is the absolute temperature of the black body in Kelvin.
