Since the reaction of ADP + Pi = ATP has a standard free energy change of -30.5 kJ/mol, the reverse reaction (ATP + P = ADP + Pi) will have a positive free energy change of +30.5 kJ/mol.

Now, we need to calculate the standard free energy change for the hydrolysis reaction at the given concentrations of ATP, ADP, and Pi. We can use the following equation:

ΔG = ΔG° + RT ln(Q)

where ΔG is the actual free energy change, ΔG° is the standard free energy change, R is the gas constant (8.314 J/mol·K), T is the temperature in Kelvin (37°C = 310 K), and Q is the reaction quotient.

