Δt' = Δt * √(1 - v^2/c^2)

Plugging in the given values:

Δt' = 3 years * √(1 - (0.6c)^2/c^2)
Δt' = 3 years * √(1 - 0.36)
Δt' = 3 years * √(0.64)
Δt' = 3 years * 0.8
Δt' = 2.4 years

So, when the clock in the cockpit shows 3 years, the time experienced on Earth is 2.4 years.

Now, we need to find the time shown on the clock in the cargo bay when it is compared to a clock on Earth. Since both clocks are on the same spaceship and moving at the same speed, they will experience the same time dilation effect. Therefore, the time shown on the clock in the loading bay will also be 3 years when the ship returns to Earth.

