The energy resolution of the measurement is limited by the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, which states that the uncertainty in energy (\(\Delta E\)) is inversely proportional to the lifetime (\(\tau\)):

\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{\hbar}{2\tau}
\]

where \(\hbar = \frac{h}{4\pi}\) is the reduced Planck constant.

For \(\tau = 2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s, the energy resolution is:

\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{1.054 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{2 \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J}
\]

Convert this energy to MeV:

\[
\Delta E \approx 5.27 \times 10^{-12} \text{ J} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}} \approx 3.3 \text{ MeV}
\]

Thus, the expected uncertainty in the measured energy is approximately 3.3 MeV.

The measured value of the Σ⁺ rest energy is 1385 MeV. The uncertainty due to the lifetime is \(\boxed{3.3 \text{ MeV}}\).

The actual measurement of the Σ⁺ rest energy is:

\[
E = 1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 1.5 \text{ MeV}
\]

